// slack_thread.proto — what more than one Slack-thread endpoint shares: the
// thread a subscription follows, its mode, the subscription itself, and a
// message read from the thread. AGENT API section.
//
// HOW THE DAEMON READS SLACK. Every read and every post runs the user's own
// `gns` command line (the one way agent-repl reaches Slack): a thread's new
// messages stream from the channel's echo pipe
// (`gns pipe read user.<login>.tools.slack.channel.<channel>.echo --follow
// --tag thread_ts:<root>`), the daemon's cursor into that pipe is durable, and
// a post is `gns slack send` with the post's blocks on stdin.
//
// HOW A WORKSPACE IS TOLD. Messages accumulate per subscription, durably, as
// they arrive. The daemon submits them as ONE digest, a prompt of origin
// conversation.v1.PromptOrigin.PROMPT_ORIGIN_SLACK_THREAD_NOTICE, once the
// thread has been quiet for a while: a short quiet period when any pending
// message reads as urgent (an imperative such as "stop", a direct address such
// as "@agent", an alarm emoji), a longer one otherwise, and never later than a
// cap counted from the oldest pending message. An urgent digest is an
// ordinary prompt, which the running turn's classifier may interject; any
// other digest is deferred, and runs as its own turn after the running one.
// A digest not yet delivered when a newer one is owed is superseded by it, so
// a workspace holds at most one digest per thread.
//
// THE WORKSPACE'S OWN POSTS ARE NEVER TOLD BACK TO IT. Every post the daemon
// makes for a subscription carries a footer naming the workspace and a nonce
// recorded before the post is sent; a message that carries one of the
// subscription's nonces is its own and is dropped as it arrives.

